while the cgi dinosaurs in the hit film  jurassic world dominion continue to  frighten moviegoers in theaters around  the country  a real dinosaur has been on display at  the sotheby's auction house here in new  york  it's a gorgosaurus a close relative of  the much bigger and better known t-rex  and it's expected to go for a jurassic  sized price between five and eight  million dollars if not more when the  bidding ends later this month tom holtz  is a professor of paleontology at the  university of maryland and the  smithsonian so gorgosaurus is a  tyrannosaur that means it's a cousin of  t-rex and if you picture t-rex it's kind  of the suv version it's big it's bulky  it's powerful gorgosaurus would be the  sports car model it's smaller it's sleek  it's faster gorgosaurus is the kind of  animal you wouldn't want to meet in a  dark alley or a brightly lit street or  pretty much anywhere  [Music]  the 22 foot long 10 foot high skeleton  was discovered in north central montana  in 2018.  the location near the judith river  formation has been giving up dinosaur  fossils for over a century but this was  a particularly spectacular find  complete or relatively complete dinosaur  skeletons are still pretty rare now  there have been some fairly complete  gorgosaurus that have been found in the  past but anytime we find one that is an  important new discovery because it gives  us more information about their anatomy  the gorgosaurus roamed what is now the  western u.s and canada during the late  cretaceous period around 70 million  years ago while it does resemble a t-rex  its behavior may have been different  because of the size of its prey many  paleontologists speculate the  gorgosaurus must have hunted in packs we  find multiple individuals of them  found together in death which is makes  us think that they may have lived  together in life or footprint sites  where we see multiple individuals that  were walking together at the same time  back in 1997 sotheby's sold the first  ever dinosaur at auction an enormous  t-rex skeleton nicknamed sue for 8.3  million dollars only time will tell if  this gorgosaurus will be able to take  down the mighty t-rex on the auction  block  and it is interesting i'm sure somebody  with a lot of money who would like to  have it in their large home might get  this but paleontologists some of them  out there are concerned because they  think you have this specimen that's in  such good shape that they could learn  from so maybe somebody with a lot of  money will donate it to a museum to a  museum or somewhere so that you get the  best of both worlds it was this is legal  there's nothing wrong with it it's just  some of the scientists out there but  when it's so rare and there's so much to  learn for history and science sake it  would be nice to have it in a museum  best of both worlds
 

